Here is a program that finds the nth number of the Fibonacci sequence:

```c++
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int Fib(int n);

int main() {
    cout << "Enter the nth Fibonacci number: ";
    int n;
    cin >> n;
    cout << "The nth Fibonacci number is " << Fib(n);
    return 0;
}

int Fib(int n) {
    if (n <= 2) {
        return 0;
    } else if (n <= 3) {
        return 1;
    } else {
        return Fib(n - 1) + Fib(n - 2);
    }
}
```</s>

This program defines a function called `Fib` that takes an integer `n` as an argument. A condition is checked to see if `n` is less than or equal to 2. If it is, the program returns `0`. If it is less than or equal to 3, the program returns `1`. Otherwise, the program recursively calls the function `Fib` passing the values `n - 1` and `n - 2` as arguments. The result of this call is added to the already calculated result of `Fib` at the previous recursive call. This process continues until the final result is obtained.</s>

